  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1125 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe post-frontal air mass from yesterday's frontal passage has\nsettled into the region through Monday, containing cooler, polar\nair. Continued elevated winds will advect more cold air into the\nregion tonight, allowing for lows to fall into the low 30s and\nupper 20s Tuesday morning. Highs will suffer from the cold\nadvection as well, only rising into the 40s and 50s through\nTuesday afternoon.\n\nZooming out to a CONUS-wide view, the upper pattern will begin to\nrapidly evolve through the next 72 hours. Two shortwave troughs\nwill eject into the Plains. One will come from the southwest with\nanother driving south out of central Canada. The southern trough\nwill reach the Mid-South first early Wednesday morning with the\nnorthern trough trailing it closely in the northern Plains. Upper\nlevel lift ahead of the broader troughing will begin to produce\nsurface troughing into the Mid-South.\n\nUnfortunately, large disparities in model guidance still permeate\nthe 18z and 00z guidance this afternoon and evening, leading to\nvastly differing solutions in terms of QPF from these systems\ndepending on the model suite. The main driver of the forecast\nuncertainty has been the evolution of the phasing of the two\ntroughs over the central CONUS. Half of the guidance has the two\ntroughs successfully merging, becoming highly amplified and\nsticking around the region for multiple days. Meanwhile, the\nother half of the operational model suite kicks the southern\ntrough into the Atlantic before the two can phase, leading to\ndrastically lower forcing for precipitation. In lieu of such poor\nforecast certainty, even at 72 hours out, we have opted towards\nthe NBM's middle of the road solution. The latest NBM forecast\ncalls for upwards of an inch of rain near the Tennessee River\nValley through Thursday, with a sharp drop-off in accumulation\nwest towards the Mississippi River. This forecast is highly\nsusceptible to rapid changes and forecast precipitation amounts\nmay change depending on which evolution becomes preferred in the\nnext day or two.\n\nAnother aspect of the mid-week system to mention is the potential\nfor wintry precipitation. Models have stubbornly clung on to\ntheir respective evolutions of the upper troughing through the\nweek, with two main camps as mentioned in the last paragraph: 1)\nphasing the troughs into a slower, more amplified system and 2) a\nfaster southern trough with most amplification taking place north\nof the region. If we are to see any wintry precipitation, camp\n1 (ECMWF/RRFS/GDPS) will need to win out. If this were to occur,\nthe system would linger long enough to keep precipitation over\nthe region into the overnight hours Wednesday and into Thursday\nmorning. With a more amplified system, temperatures may only\nclimb above 0 C close to the surface, allowing for some snow to\nmake its way to the ground. This only appears to occur over\nnortheastern Arkansas, the Missouri Bootheel, and potentially\nnorthern portions of West Tennessee. However, there would likely\nbe no impacts given warm ground temperatures and above freezing\nsurface temperatures. So, given the highly variable forecast and\nthe lack of expected impacts, have opted for the NBM solution on\nprecipitation type as well with little to no snow in the current\nforecast.\n\nRegardless of the upper systems evolution, the leftover pattern\nwill quickly kick the broader troughing out of the CONUS and into\nthe Atlantic by this upcoming weekend. Surprisingly, especially\ngiven the insane amount of model inconsistencies, ensembles seem\nto have a good handle on the upper pattern through next weekend.\nNorthwesterlies will take over with surface high pressure over\nthe southeastern CONUS. Therefore, the forecast is expected to\ndry out by Friday and remain this way through Sunday with highs\nclimbing back into the 50s and 60s.",
